{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=(2-3x)^{3/2}$ about $x=0$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-8269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})(\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-8269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a433e0608a9d2d23a903bc7da3e7e98dc3b0f7844bb3ffd4a203375a5292848a"} {"question" "q-8269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-8269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{9}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}"] ["math" "1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{2}^{1/2}(1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})"] ["math" "{2}^{3/2}(1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})"] ["math" "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-8269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{\\pi x/2}$ about $x=0$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot x+(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}x+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a433e0608a9d2d23a903bc7da3e7e98dc3b0f7844bb3ffd4a203375a5292848a"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}x+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{x}^{2}"] ["math" "1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}x+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}{x}^{2}"] ["math" "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}{x}^{4}"] ["math" "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}x-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{3}}{8}{x}^{2}"] ["math" "x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-12383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}x+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x}$ about $x=5$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=5,$ we plug $a=5$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(5)+{f}^{′}(5)(x-5)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(5)(x-5)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(5),$ ${f}^{′}(5),$ and ${f}^{″}(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{x} & ⟹ &  & f(5) & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(5) & =-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{x}^{-3} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(5) & =\\frac{2}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(5),$ ${f}^{′}(5),$ and ${f}^{″}(5)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =(\\frac{6}{5})+(-\\frac{1}{25})\\cdot (x-5)+(\\frac{2}{125})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-5)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{25}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{125}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a433e0608a9d2d23a903bc7da3e7e98dc3b0f7844bb3ffd4a203375a5292848a"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{12}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{24}(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{25}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{125}(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{25}(x-5)+\\frac{2}{25}(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}(x-5)+\\frac{2}{125}(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{6}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{24}(x-5)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-15638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{25}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{125}(x-5)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a433e0608a9d2d23a903bc7da3e7e98dc3b0f7844bb3ffd4a203375a5292848a"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-81059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{1}{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})(x-\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})=-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4})=-{\\pi }^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2})}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a433e0608a9d2d23a903bc7da3e7e98dc3b0f7844bb3ffd4a203375a5292848a"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"} {"question" "q-83722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038371/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae5e3453e79a9d83ae09934e6daace6835ef696ce4f9a4b15d5523f0b70bcf58"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1300 "ret